template <typename T>
T max_value(T a, T b) {
return (a > b) ? a : b;
}
max_value(3, 4); // T deduced as int
max_value(3.5, 2.1); // T deduced as double
max_value(std::string{"abc"}, std::string{"abd"}); // T as std::string
The compiler generates a separate version of the function for each T you use. This is compile-time code generation — no runtime cost.

template <typename T, typename U>
auto add(T a, U b) {
return a + b;
}
add(3, 4.5); // returns double

template <typename T>
class Box {
T value_;
public:
Box(T v) : value_(std::move(v)) {}
const T& get() const { return value_; }
void set(T v) { value_ = std::move(v); }
};
Box<int> bi(5);
Box<std::string> bs("hello");
Each Box<T> is a distinct type generated at compile time.
C++17 added CTAD (class template argument deduction):
Box b1(5); // deduces Box<int>
Box b2(std::string{"x"}); // deduces Box<std::string>

#include <vector>
std::vector<int> v;
v.push_back(10);
v.push_back(20);
v.push_back(30);
v[0]; // 10 — no bounds check
v.at(0); // 10 — throws if out of bounds
v.size(); // 3
v.empty(); // false
v.front(); // 10
v.back(); // 30
v.clear(); // empty
std::vector<int> w{1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
w.pop_back(); // remove last
w.insert(w.begin() + 1, 99); // insert at position
w.erase(w.begin()); // remove first
for (int x : w) std::cout << x << " ";
Internally a dynamic array. Amortised O(1) push_back, O(1) random access, O(n) insert/erase in middle.
Trick: if you know the final size, v.reserve(N) ahead of time to prevent reallocations.

